# From Noisy Data to Causal Claims

## An Interactive Guide to Bayesian Structural Time Series

You measure something over time — website visits, product demand, hospital admissions, server
latency. The numbers go up and down. Then *something happens*: a marketing campaign launches, a
new policy takes effect, a server gets upgraded. The numbers change. But did they change *because*
of the intervention, or would they have changed anyway?

This is the central question that Bayesian Structural Time Series (BSTS) answers. By the end of
this notebook you will understand — intuitively, visually, and mathematically — how the algorithm
works, why it works, and how to apply it across five completely different domains.

**What you'll be able to do after this notebook:**

* Decompose any time series into trend, seasonality, and noise
* Estimate causal effects of interventions with honest uncertainty
* Forecast future values with calibrated prediction intervals
* Detect anomalies using statistical process control
* Split credit fairly across overlapping campaigns
* Validate your results before trusting them

**Who this is for:** Technically curious people — engineers, data scientists, product managers,
researchers — who want to *understand* the algorithm, not just call an API. We assume comfort
with code and basic probability, but no prior knowledge of state-space models or Bayesian inference.

## Setup

