## Part IV: Five Domains, One Engine

The same decompose-project-compare pattern applies across completely different fields. Each
case study below is self-contained — it generates its own data, runs the analysis, and shows
the result. The underlying engine is identical; only the *framing* changes.

### 12. Case Study: Did our email campaign drive conversions?

**Context:** You run a 2-week email campaign for an e-commerce site. Daily conversion counts
are available for 30 days before and 14 days during the campaign. Did the campaign actually
lift conversions, or did they rise for seasonal reasons?

```elixir
Guide.seed!(3001)

# Generate synthetic daily conversions with weekly seasonality
n_pre_days = 30
n_post_days = 14
total_days = n_pre_days + n_post_days

# Baseline: 200 conversions/day with weekly pattern and slight trend
daily_baseline =
  for d <- 0..(total_days - 1) do
    200.0 + 0.8 * d + 15.0 * :math.sin(2 * :math.pi * d / 7) + :rand.normal() * 8.0
  end

# True campaign effect: +25 conversions/day during post-period
true_campaign_effect = 25.0

daily_obs =
  daily_baseline
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.map(fn {b, i} ->
    if i >= n_pre_days, do: b + true_campaign_effect + :rand.normal() * 3.0, else: b
  end)

Guide.series_plot(
  %{"daily conversions" => daily_obs},
  title: "E-commerce conversions — did the email campaign work?",
  intervention_at: n_pre_days,
  x_title: "day",
  y_title: "conversions"
)
```

```elixir
campaign = %{
  name: "summer_email_blast",
  channel: :email,
  start_index: n_pre_days + 1,
  end_index: total_days,
  baseline_start: 1,
  baseline_end: n_pre_days
}

marketing_result =
  MarketingLift.measure_lift(daily_obs, campaign,
    seasonality: 7,
    num_samples: 30,
    burn_in: 15,
    seed: 700
  )

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Marketing lift result

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Cumulative lift | #{Float.round(marketing_result.effect.cumulative, 1)} conversions |
| True cumulative lift | #{Float.round(true_campaign_effect * n_post_days, 1)} conversions |
| Significant? | #{if marketing_result.significant?, do: "Yes ✓", else: "No ✗"} |
| Relative lift | #{Float.round(marketing_result.effect.relative * 100, 1)}% |

The model estimates a #{Float.round(marketing_result.effect.relative * 100, 1)}% lift.
The true effect was #{true_campaign_effect} conversions/day × #{n_post_days} days =
#{Float.round(true_campaign_effect * n_post_days, 1)} total.
""")
```

### 13. Case Study: How much ice cream should we stock?

**Context:** You manage inventory for an ice cream distributor. You have 120 days of historical
demand data and need to forecast the next 14 days to decide how much to order. Getting the
point forecast right isn't enough — you need to know the *range* of plausible demand to
compute safety stock that avoids stockouts 95% of the time.

```elixir
Guide.seed!(4001)

# Generate demand with strong weekly seasonality and an upward trend
demand_history =
  for d <- 0..119 do
    base = 500.0 + 2.0 * d
    seasonal = 80.0 * :math.sin(2 * :math.pi * d / 7) + 30.0 * :math.cos(2 * :math.pi * d / 7)
    base + seasonal + :rand.normal() * 25.0
  end

# True future (we'll use this to check forecast quality)
demand_future =
  for d <- 120..133 do
    base = 500.0 + 2.0 * d
    seasonal = 80.0 * :math.sin(2 * :math.pi * d / 7) + 30.0 * :math.cos(2 * :math.pi * d / 7)
    base + seasonal + :rand.normal() * 25.0
  end

forecast = DemandForecaster.forecast(demand_history,
  horizon: 14,
  seasonality: 7,
  num_samples: 40,
  burn_in: 20,
  seed: 16
)

# Compute safety stock for 95% service level, 7-day lead time
safety = DemandForecaster.safety_stock(forecast, service_level: 0.95, lead_time: 7)

# Visualize forecast vs. actual
band_rows =
  0..13
  |> Enum.map(fn t ->
    %{
      t: t,
      mean: Enum.at(forecast.mean, t),
      lower: Enum.at(forecast.lower, t),
      upper: Enum.at(forecast.upper, t)
    }
  end)

Guide.band_plot(band_rows,
  title: "14-day demand forecast (blue band) vs. actual demand (red)",
  actual: demand_future
)
```

```elixir
# Forecast quality metrics
forecast_mae = Guide.mae(demand_future, forecast.mean)

coverage =
  Enum.zip(demand_future, Enum.zip(forecast.lower, forecast.upper))
  |> Enum.count(fn {actual, {lo, hi}} -> actual >= lo and actual <= hi end)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Demand forecast evaluation

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| MAE | #{Float.round(forecast_mae, 1)} units/day |
| Interval coverage | #{coverage}/#{length(demand_future)} days (#{Float.round(100 * coverage / length(demand_future), 0)}%) |
| Safety stock (7-day lead time, 95% SL) | **#{Float.round(safety.total, 0)} units** |

The safety stock is computed from the *upper quantile* of the forecast distribution,
not the mean. This is why Bayesian forecasting is valuable for operations: the
uncertainty is built into the decision, not bolted on as an afterthought.
""")
```

### 14. Case Study: Is this server spike an anomaly?

**Context:** You monitor a web service. You have 96 hours of normal baseline traffic, then
a new 24-hour monitoring window opens. Two genuine anomalies occur — a traffic spike at
hour 8 and a dip at hour 16. Can the detector find them without flooding you with false alarms?

```elixir
normal_history = Enum.take(obs, 96)
stream = Enum.take(Enum.drop(obs, 96), 24)

# Inject two synthetic anomalies
stream_with_anomalies =
  stream
  |> List.update_at(8, &(&1 + 35.0))   # spike
  |> List.update_at(16, &(&1 - 30.0))  # dip

# Fit the detector on normal history
detector = AnomalyDetector.fit(normal_history, method: :filter, alpha: 0.01)

# Score the new window
scores = AnomalyDetector.score(detector, stream_with_anomalies)
summary = AnomalyDetector.summary(scores)
```

```elixir
# Plot the monitoring stream with anomalies highlighted
z_scores = Enum.map(scores, &abs(&1.z_score))
threshold = BstsNx.Utils.z_score(0.01)

z_data =
  z_scores
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.flat_map(fn {z, t} ->
    [
      %{"series" => "|z-score|", "t" => t, "value" => z},
      %{"series" => "threshold (α=0.01)", "t" => t, "value" => threshold}
    ]
  end)

Vl.new(width: 900, height: 300, title: "Anomaly detection: z-scores vs. threshold")
|> Vl.data_from_values(z_data)
|> Vl.mark(:line)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "t", type: :quantitative, title: "hour")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "value", type: :quantitative, title: "|z-score|")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "series", type: :nominal, legend: [orient: :bottom])
|> Vl.encode_field(:stroke_dash, "series", type: :nominal)
```

```elixir
flagged_indices =
  scores
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.filter(fn {s, _} -> s.anomaly? end)
  |> Enum.map(fn {s, i} -> %{hour: i, z_score: Float.round(abs(s.z_score), 2), severity: s.severity} end)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Detection results

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Anomalies flagged | #{summary.anomalies} |
| Injected anomalies | 2 (at hours 8 and 16) |
| False alarms | #{max(summary.anomalies - 2, 0)} |

The detector flags points where the z-score exceeds the threshold. At α=0.01,
roughly 1% of normal observations would be falsely flagged. The two injected
anomalies should stand out clearly.
""")
```

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(flagged_indices)
```

### 15. Case Study: Did the new speed limit reduce accidents?

**Context:** A city lowered speed limits on major roads starting in month 37. You have monthly
accident counts for 60 months total (36 before, 24 after). This is a classic **interrupted time
series (ITS)** design used in public health and policy evaluation.

The statistical question is identical to causal impact: estimate the counterfactual
("what would accident rates have been without the policy?") and compare it to the actual
post-policy data. The framing is different — instead of marketing lift, we're measuring
policy effect — but the engine is the same.

```elixir
Guide.seed!(5001)

# Generate monthly accidents with a trend and seasonality
n_pre_months = 36
n_post_months = 24
total_months = n_pre_months + n_post_months
true_policy_effect = -8.0  # reduction of 8 accidents/month

monthly_baseline =
  for m <- 0..(total_months - 1) do
    base = 45.0 - 0.1 * m  # slight natural decline
    seasonal = 5.0 * :math.sin(2 * :math.pi * m / 12)
    base + seasonal + :rand.normal() * 3.0
  end

monthly_obs =
  monthly_baseline
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.map(fn {b, i} ->
    if i >= n_pre_months, do: b + true_policy_effect + :rand.normal() * 2.0, else: b
  end)

Guide.series_plot(
  %{"monthly accidents" => monthly_obs},
  title: "Monthly traffic accidents — speed limit changed at month 37",
  intervention_at: n_pre_months,
  x_title: "month",
  y_title: "accidents"
)
```

```elixir
intervention = %{
  intervention_name: "speed_limit_reduction",
  intervention_date_index: n_pre_months + 1,
  pre_period_start: 1,
  outcome_metric: "accidents per month"
}

policy_result =
  PolicyEvaluator.evaluate(monthly_obs, intervention,
    post_period_end: total_months,
    seasonality: 12,
    num_samples: 30,
    burn_in: 15,
    seed: 701
  )

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Policy evaluation result

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Cumulative effect | #{Float.round(policy_result.effect.cumulative, 1)} accidents |
| Per-period effect | #{Float.round(policy_result.effect.per_period, 1)} accidents/month |
| True effect | #{true_policy_effect} accidents/month |
| Significant? | #{if policy_result.significant?, do: "Yes ✓", else: "No ✗"} |

The model estimates the speed limit reduction prevented approximately
#{Float.round(abs(policy_result.effect.cumulative), 0)} accidents over
#{n_post_months} months. The true effect was #{abs(true_policy_effect)} fewer
accidents per month × #{n_post_months} months = #{abs(true_policy_effect * n_post_months)}.
""")
```

### 16. Case Study: Which TV spots earned their airtime?

**Context:** This brings together everything from Parts II-III in a single production-ready
call. Four TV spots aired during a championship game. Leadership wants to know: what was the
total lift, and which spots get credit?

```elixir
tv_result =
  TVAttribution.attribute(
    obs, spots_post,
    pre_period: pre_period,
    post_period: post_period,
    seasonality: 24,
    num_samples: 30,
    burn_in: 15,
    seed: 501,
    alpha: 0.05
  )

# Attribution bar chart
tv_attr_rows =
  Enum.map(tv_result.spots, fn s ->
    %{label: s.spot_id, value: s.lift, lower: s[:ci_lower] || s.lift, upper: s[:ci_upper] || s.lift}
  end)

Guide.attribution_chart(tv_attr_rows,
  title: "TV attribution: lift by spot with credible intervals"
)
```

```elixir
Kino.Markdown.new("""
### TV attribution summary

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Total lift | #{Float.round(tv_result.total_lift, 1)} sessions |
| Total lift SD | #{Float.round(tv_result.total_lift_sd, 1)} |
| True total lift | #{Float.round(true_post_lift, 1)} sessions |
| Overlap groups | #{inspect(tv_result.overlaps)} |

Spots in the same overlap group have their credit allocated via Shapley values,
ensuring fair attribution even when air windows overlap.
""")
```

