## Part V: Trust But Verify

### 17. Covariate selection: choosing the right controls

Including irrelevant controls adds noise; including too many leads to overfitting. Covariate
selection screens candidates by their correlation with the target during the pre-period.

```elixir
target_pre = Enum.take(obs, 96)

candidate_matrix =
  controls
  |> Enum.map(&Enum.take(&1, 96))
  |> Nx.tensor()
  |> Nx.transpose()

selection = CovariateSelection.select(target_pre, candidate_matrix,
  threshold: 0.15,
  max_controls: 2
)

corr_rows =
  Enum.map(selection.correlations, fn {idx, corr} ->
    %{candidate: "control_#{idx}", correlation: Float.round(corr, 3), selected?: idx in selection.selected_indices}
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(corr_rows)
```

### 18. Breaking your own results: five diagnostic checks

Before publishing any causal estimate, try to break it. These five checks catch the most
common failure modes:

```elixir
post_obs_list = Enum.slice(obs, 96..143)

{rolling_cf, rolling_fit} =
  RollingBaseline.fit_and_predict(
    Enum.take(obs, 96),
    48,
    num_seasons: 24,
    num_samples: 30,
    burn_in: 15,
    seed: 500
  )

actual_t = Nx.tensor(post_obs_list)
baseline_t = Nx.tensor(rolling_cf.mean)
state_var_t = Nx.tensor(rolling_cf.variance)
indices = Enum.to_list(0..(length(post_obs_list) - 1))

estimate_fn = fn sessions, idxs ->
  vals = Enum.map(idxs, &Enum.at(sessions, &1))
  lift = Enum.sum(vals) - length(vals) * (Enum.sum(sessions) / length(sessions))
  sd = max(abs(lift) * 0.1, 1.0)

  %{
    lift_sessions: lift,
    lift_pct: lift / max(Enum.sum(sessions), 1.0),
    lift_ci95: %{lower: lift - 1.96 * sd, upper: lift + 1.96 * sd, sd: sd}
  }
end

evaluation =
  Validation.evaluate(%{
    actual: actual_t,
    baseline: baseline_t,
    indices: indices,
    coverage: %{state_variances: state_var_t, obs_variance: rolling_cf.obs_variance},
    placebo: %{
      sessions: post_obs_list,
      on_air_indices: Enum.to_list(5..12),
      estimate_fn: estimate_fn
    },
    effect_stability: %{
      main_lift: summary_structured.cumulative_effect.mean,
      estimate_at_window_fn: fn window ->
        h = max(1, div(window, 60))
        post_slice = Enum.take(post_obs_list, h)
        base_slice = Enum.take(counterfactual_mean, h)
        Enum.zip(post_slice, base_slice) |> Enum.reduce(0.0, fn {a, b}, acc -> acc + (a - b) end)
      end,
      window: 120,
      delta: 30
    }
  })

assessment = evaluation.verdicts
checks_satisfied? = Enum.all?(assessment, fn {_k, v} -> v in [:pass, :skip] end)
```

```elixir
Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Validation dashboard

| Check | What it tests | Result |
|-------|--------------|--------|
| Prediction error | Does the baseline predict well out-of-sample? | #{Map.get(assessment, :prediction_error, :skip)} |
| Coverage | Do credible intervals contain actuals? | #{Map.get(assessment, :coverage, :skip)} |
| Durbin-Watson | Are residuals autocorrelated? (want ~2.0) | #{Map.get(assessment, :durbin_watson, :skip)} |
| Placebo | Does the method find false effects in null periods? | #{Map.get(assessment, :placebo, :skip)} |
| Stability | Does the effect estimate stabilize? | #{Map.get(assessment, :effect_stability, :skip)} |

**Requested checks satisfied?** #{if checks_satisfied?, do: "Yes — requested checks pass or were skipped", else: "No — investigate warnings and failures"}
Skipped checks supply no evidence; this is not a release-readiness decision.
""")
```

#### Calibration: can we recover a known lift?

The ultimate sanity check. Generate completely fresh data with a known lift, run the full
estimator, and check whether the credible interval covers the truth.

```elixir
calibration =
  Validation.known_lift_injection(
    40.0,
    Components.local_level_spec(),
    n_pre: 60,
    n_post: 20,
    num_samples: 40,
    burn_in: 20,
    seed: 2026,
    credible_level: 0.95
  )

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Calibration result

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| True injected lift | #{calibration.true_lift} |
| Estimated mean | #{Float.round(calibration.estimated_mean, 1)} |
| 95% CI covers truth? | #{if calibration.covered, do: "Yes ✓", else: "No ✗"} |
| Relative error | #{Float.round(100 * calibration.relative_error, 1)}% |

A well-calibrated method should cover the true value ~95% of the time when using 95% intervals.
""")
```

---

## Part VI: Forecasting Beyond Causal Inference

### 19. Bayesian forecasting with credible intervals

Causal impact asks "what *would have* happened?" Forecasting asks "what *will* happen?" Same
engine, different direction. The posterior draws from the MCMC sampler each produce a different
future trajectory, giving calibrated prediction intervals.

```elixir
train = Enum.take(obs, 120)
test = Enum.drop(obs, 120)

forecast =
  Forecaster.fit_predict(train, length(test),
    seasonality: 24,
    num_samples: 40,
    burn_in: 20,
    seed: 15
  )

band_rows =
  0..(length(test) - 1)
  |> Enum.map(fn t ->
    %{t: t, mean: Enum.at(forecast.mean, t), lower: Enum.at(forecast.lower, t), upper: Enum.at(forecast.upper, t)}
  end)

Guide.band_plot(band_rows,
  title: "24-hour forecast: prediction band vs. actual",
  actual: test
)
```

```elixir
forecast_mae = Guide.mae(test, forecast.mean)

fc_coverage =
  Enum.zip(test, Enum.zip(forecast.lower, forecast.upper))
  |> Enum.count(fn {a, {lo, hi}} -> a >= lo and a <= hi end)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Forecast evaluation

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| MAE | #{Float.round(forecast_mae, 2)} |
| Interval coverage | #{fc_coverage}/#{length(test)} (#{Float.round(100 * fc_coverage / length(test), 0)}%) |

Well-calibrated 95% intervals should contain ~95% of actual values.
""")
```

#### AR baseline comparison

For context, here's a simpler autoregressive (AR) model. AR(p) predicts the next value as a
weighted combination of the p most recent values. No structural decomposition, no seasonality
modeling — just recent history.

```elixir
ar_forecast =
  BstsNx.BCT.ARForecaster.fit_predict(train, length(test),
    order: 2,
    num_samples: 200,
    seed: 17
  )

ar_mae = Guide.mae(test, ar_forecast.mean)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### BSTS vs. AR(2) comparison

| Model | MAE |
|-------|-----|
| BSTS (trend + seasonal) | #{Float.round(forecast_mae, 2)} |
| AR(2) | #{Float.round(ar_mae, 2)} |

BSTS typically outperforms simple AR models when the data has clear structural patterns
(trend, seasonality). AR models can be competitive for short horizons when autocorrelation
is the dominant signal.
""")
```

---

## Epilogue

### 20. When to use what: a decision guide

```elixir
Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Which API for which question?

| Question | Module | Key function |
|----------|--------|-------------|
| "Did this intervention have a causal effect?" | `CausalImpact` | `estimate/4` or `estimate_structured/5` |
| "Just tell me if it was significant" | `InterventionAnalysis` | `analyze/3` |
| "Which marketing channel drove the most lift?" | `MarketingLift` | `measure_lift/3` |
| "Did this policy change work?" | `PolicyEvaluator` | `evaluate/3` |
| "Which TV spots deserve credit?" | `TVAttribution` | `attribute/3` |
| "How much should we order next week?" | `DemandForecaster` | `forecast/2` + `safety_stock/2` |
| "What will happen in the next N periods?" | `Forecaster` | `fit_predict/3` |
| "Is this data point abnormal?" | `AnomalyDetector` | `fit/2` + `score/2` |
| "Causal impact + attribution in one call" | `Pipeline` | `run/6` |
| "Can I trust these results?" | `Validation` | `assess/1` |

### How complexity flows

```

Components ──► ModelSpec ──► GibbsSampler ──► CausalImpact ──► Pipeline

```
│              │              │               │               │
└──► compose   └──► specs     └──► posterior   └──► effect     └──► attribution
```

```

Every high-level API ultimately calls the same statistical engine. Understanding
the engine (Parts II-III) lets you diagnose problems and tune parameters in any
domain application (Part IV).
""")
```

### 21. API reference

```elixir
modules = [
  BstsNx.KalmanFilter, BstsNx.Smoother, BstsNx.GibbsSampler,
  BstsNx.Components, BstsNx.CausalImpact, BstsNx.InterventionAnalysis,
  BstsNx.Forecaster, BstsNx.Pipeline,
  BstsNx.Applications.AnomalyDetector, BstsNx.Applications.DemandForecaster,
  BstsNx.Applications.MarketingLift, BstsNx.Applications.PolicyEvaluator,
  BstsNx.Applications.TVAttribution,
  BstsNx.SpotAttributor, BstsNx.ShapleyAllocator,
  BstsNx.CovariateSelection, BstsNx.Validation, BstsNx.Diagnostics,
  BstsNx.Synthetic.Generator, BstsNx.Synthetic.Adstock
]

rows =
  for mod <- modules,
      {fun, arity} <- mod.__info__(:functions),
      fun not in [:module_info, :__info__] do
    %{module: inspect(mod), function: "#{fun}/#{arity}"}
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(rows)
```

### 22. Exercises for the curious

1. **Sensitivity analysis**: Change `config.effect.coefficient` from 12.0 to 5.0. How does
   the causal impact estimate and its uncertainty change? At what effect size does the signal
   become indistinguishable from noise?

2. **Model complexity**: Run the causal impact estimator with just a local level model, then
   with trend + seasonality, then with trend + seasonality + controls. Compare recovery
   accuracy. When does added complexity help vs. hurt?

3. **Speed vs. accuracy**: Replace `method: :mcmc` with `method: :filter` in the intervention
   analysis. How much faster is it? How much accuracy do you lose?

4. **Overlapping spots**: Edit the spot definitions to create more overlap. How do the Shapley
   allocations change? What happens when all spots have identical windows?

5. **Anomaly injection**: Add anomalies at different magnitudes (5, 10, 20, 50 units above
   baseline). At what magnitude does the detector reliably flag them? How does the alpha
   threshold affect the trade-off between detection and false alarms?

6. **Calibration sweep**: Run `known_lift_injection` with true lifts of 10, 20, 40, 80, 160.
   Plot coverage vs. true lift. Is the method better calibrated for large or small effects?

### 23. What you've learned

| Section | Concept              | Key insight                                                                          |
| ------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| 1-2     | The problem          | Raw data is signal + noise; your eyes can't tell them apart                          |
| 3       | Synthetic data       | Test on known truth before trusting on real data                                     |
| 4       | Adstock + saturation | Marketing effects carry over and diminish — domain knowledge matters               |
| 5       | Kalman filter        | Optimal online blending of model prediction and noisy observation                    |
| 6       | RTS smoother         | Hindsight (backward pass) always improves estimates                                  |
| 7       | Gibbs sampler        | When you don't know the parameters, alternate: sample states, then sample parameters |
| 8-9     | Causal impact        | Fit pre-period → project counterfactual → subtract from actual                   |
| 10-11   | Shapley attribution  | Fair credit allocation across overlapping interventions                              |
| 12      | Marketing lift       | Same engine, marketing framing                                                       |
| 13      | Demand forecasting   | Posterior intervals → safety stock that accounts for uncertainty                   |
| 14      | Anomaly detection    | Kalman prediction errors as calibrated anomaly scores                                |
| 15      | Policy evaluation    | Interrupted time series = causal impact for policy                                   |
| 16      | TV attribution       | Full pipeline: model → counterfactual → Shapley → per-spot credit              |
| 17-18   | Validation           | Placebo tests, coverage checks, calibration — break your results before publishing |

The unifying idea: **decompose** the series into structure + noise, **project** the structure
forward to build a counterfactual or forecast, and **compare** the projection against reality
to measure effects, detect anomalies, or quantify uncertainty.

Every section used the same underlying engine. The only thing that changed was the question.
